<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elbe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Elbe is a major river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, about 1,100 km long, rising in the Giant Mountains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and flowing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>. In Roman times it marked the limit of Augustus's attempted conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>, and in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the frontier between Germans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. It linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> to the sea.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elbe.jpg" alt="Elbe" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: che · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Labe_udoli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
